1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:00,719
Hello!

2
00:00:00,719 --> 00:00:06,092
This week you will learn to recognise the four Writing Task 2 question types,

3
00:00:06,092 --> 00:00:09,790
write an introduction with a paraphrase and a thesis,

4
00:00:09,790 --> 00:00:13,140
and write a conclusion without adding new ideas.

5
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:18,014
A well-organised Task 2 essay has four paragraphs:

6
00:00:18,014 --> 00:00:21,946
an introduction, two body paragraphs, and a conclusion.

7
00:00:21,946 --> 00:00:25,020
Write at least two hundred and fifty words.

8
00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:31,311
A suggested split of the forty minutes is five minutes planning, thirty minutes writing,

9
00:00:31,311 --> 00:00:33,169
and five minutes checking.

10
00:00:33,169 --> 00:00:37,816
Each body paragraph still follows the TEEL model from lesson one.

11
00:00:39,116 --> 00:00:41,194
There are four question types:

12
00:00:41,194 --> 00:00:44,795
opinion, discussion, problem-solution, and two-part.

13
00:00:44,795 --> 00:00:48,466
Read the final instruction of the question carefully,

14
00:00:48,466 --> 00:00:51,236
because it decides your essay structure.

15
00:00:51,236 --> 00:00:56,084
Leaving out part of the question is the biggest task response mistake.

16
00:00:57,384 --> 00:00:57,997
Opinion:

17
00:00:57,997 --> 00:01:01,059
to what extent do you agree or disagree?

18
00:01:01,059 --> 00:01:01,901
Discussion:

19
00:01:01,901 --> 00:01:05,041
discuss both views and give your opinion.

20
00:01:05,041 --> 00:01:06,649
Problem and solution:

21
00:01:06,649 --> 00:01:09,864
what are the causes, and what can be done?

22
00:01:09,864 --> 00:01:10,553
Two-part:

23
00:01:10,553 --> 00:01:14,688
why is this happening, and is it positive or negative?

24
00:01:17,988 --> 00:01:20,104
An introduction has two parts.

25
00:01:20,104 --> 00:01:24,053
Sentence one paraphrases the question in your own words:

26
00:01:24,053 --> 00:01:28,989
use synonyms, change the word class, or change the sentence structure.

27
00:01:28,989 --> 00:01:34,560
Sentence two is your thesis, which states your position or how you will answer.

28
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:38,580
Don't copy the question, and don't write a vague opening.

29
00:01:39,880 --> 00:01:41,017
The prompt says:

30
00:01:41,017 --> 00:01:44,215
Every student should learn to cook at school.

31
00:01:44,215 --> 00:01:45,139
A paraphrase:

32
00:01:45,139 --> 00:01:49,048
Schools ought to teach all pupils how to prepare meals.

33
00:01:52,348 --> 00:01:54,412
A thesis for an opinion essay:

34
00:01:54,412 --> 00:02:00,124
I largely agree, because cooking is a practical skill that supports healthy living.

35
00:02:03,424 --> 00:02:05,906
A conclusion needs only two sentences.

36
00:02:05,906 --> 00:02:09,172
Start with a linking phrase such as in conclusion,

37
00:02:09,172 --> 00:02:13,026
restate your view in different words from the introduction,

38
00:02:13,026 --> 00:02:18,840
and then summarise your reasons or give a recommendation directly connected to your view.

39
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:23,608
If an idea is not in the body paragraphs, don't put it in the conclusion.

40
00:02:24,908 --> 00:02:30,696
In conclusion, teaching cooking in schools gives pupils a practical life skill.

41
00:02:30,696 --> 00:02:34,652
Schools should therefore offer short, regular lessons.

42
00:02:37,952 --> 00:02:40,935
Body paragraphs follow the question type.

43
00:02:40,935 --> 00:02:46,173
For discussion, give one view per paragraph, and state your own opinion.

44
00:02:46,173 --> 00:02:51,556
For two-part questions, write one paragraph per question, and answer both.

45
00:02:51,556 --> 00:02:57,522
For problem-solution, paragraph one describes the problems and paragraph two gives

46
00:02:57,522 --> 00:02:58,904
matching solutions.

47
00:03:00,204 --> 00:03:03,061
The benefits outweigh the drawbacks.

48
00:03:03,061 --> 00:03:06,314
Heavy traffic contributes to the problem.

49
00:03:06,314 --> 00:03:09,012
Governments must tackle the issue.

50
00:03:12,312 --> 00:03:14,045
Four common mistakes.

51
00:03:14,045 --> 00:03:14,375
One:

52
00:03:14,375 --> 00:03:18,996
copying the question word for word as your introduction.

53
00:03:18,996 --> 00:03:19,326
Two:

54
00:03:19,326 --> 00:03:21,224
having no clear thesis.

55
00:03:21,224 --> 00:03:21,719
Three:

56
00:03:21,719 --> 00:03:26,917
missing part of the question, especially in two-part questions.

57
00:03:26,917 --> 00:03:27,330
Four:

58
00:03:27,330 --> 00:03:30,218
adding new ideas in the conclusion.

59
00:03:30,218 --> 00:03:36,984
Also, don't begin every sentence mechanically with firstly, secondly, or moreover.

60
00:03:38,284 --> 00:03:40,373
Mini quiz, three questions.

61
00:03:40,373 --> 00:03:47,181
Listen to each prompt and decide whether it is an opinion, discussion, problem-solution,

62
00:03:47,181 --> 00:03:48,806
or two-part question.

63
00:03:48,806 --> 00:03:51,436
The answers are in the next scene.

64
00:03:52,736 --> 00:03:53,074
One.

65
00:03:53,074 --> 00:03:57,629
Public parks are more important than shopping centres.

66
00:03:57,629 --> 00:04:01,003
To what extent do you agree or disagree?

67
00:04:01,003 --> 00:04:01,340
Two.

68
00:04:01,340 --> 00:04:05,727
Plastic waste is a growing problem in coastal towns.

69
00:04:05,727 --> 00:04:10,451
What problems does it cause, and how can they be solved?

70
00:04:10,451 --> 00:04:10,957
Three.

71
00:04:10,957 --> 00:04:16,777
Many people now eat in restaurants more often than they cook at home.

72
00:04:16,777 --> 00:04:17,790
Why is this?

73
00:04:17,790 --> 00:04:21,248
Is it a positive or negative development?

74
00:04:27,548 --> 00:04:28,130
Answers.

75
00:04:28,130 --> 00:04:28,421
One:

76
00:04:28,421 --> 00:04:33,151
opinion, because it asks to what extent do you agree or disagree.

77
00:04:33,151 --> 00:04:33,442
Two:

78
00:04:33,442 --> 00:04:39,045
problem-solution, because it asks about both the problem and how to solve it.

79
00:04:39,045 --> 00:04:39,482
Three:

80
00:04:39,482 --> 00:04:44,648
two-part, because it has two separate questions, why this is happening,

81
00:04:44,648 --> 00:04:50,324
and whether it is a positive or negative development, so you must answer both.

82
00:04:52,624 --> 00:04:53,164
Summary:

83
00:04:53,164 --> 00:04:58,629
identify the question type, write an introduction with a paraphrase and a thesis,

84
00:04:58,629 --> 00:05:04,499
cover every part of the question in the body, and write a conclusion with no new ideas.

85
00:05:04,499 --> 00:05:06,659
Do the ten exercises in the app,

86
00:05:06,659 --> 00:05:10,909
then write a two-hundred-and-fifty-word essay in forty minutes.

87
00:05:10,909 --> 00:05:16,982
After that, mark it yourself on the four criteria and rewrite your weakest body paragraph.

88
00:05:16,982 --> 00:05:22,312
Your self-marked score is only an estimate, and it doesn't replace a real exam.
